gras du Mézenc","dish/fin-gras-du-mezenc"],["Flammekueche","dish/flammekueche"],["Foie gras","dish/foie-gras"],["Foin de Crau","dish/foin-de-crau"],["Fraisier","dish/fraisier"],["French onion soup","dish/french-onion-soup"],["French restaurant","dish/french-restaurant"],["Fricassee","dish/fricassee"],["Garbure","dish/garbure"],["Gâteau Basque","dish/gateau-basque"],["Gugelhupf","dish/gugelhupf"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/f1/02014._Verschiedene_Canap%C3%A9s%2C_Sanok.JPG/500px-02014._Verschiedene_Canap%C3%A9s%2C_Sanok.JPG","w":500,"h":582,"by":"Silar","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:02014._Verschiedene_Canap%C3%A9s%2C_Sanok.JPG"}},"dish/clootie-dumpling":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/clootie-dumpling","n":"Clootie dumpling","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Scottish cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["A clootie dumpling is a traditional Scottish pudding made with flour, breadcrumbs, dried fruit (currants, raisins, sultanas), suet, sugar and spices with some milk to bind it. Ingredients are mixed well into a dough, then wrapped up in a floured cloth (the clootie), placed in a large pan of boiling water and simmered for a few hours before being lifted out and dried near the fire or in an oven. Recipes vary from region to region."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-clootie-dumpling"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clootie_dumpling"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Scottish cuisine","cuisine/scottish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Dried fruit","ingredient/dried-fruit"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Suet","ingredient/suet"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Scottish Scran","ref":"site/scottishscran-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"scottishscran.com","note":"Blog working through traditional Scottish dishes one recipe at a time.","url":"https://scottishscran.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Something Sweet Something Savoury: Scottish recipes","ref":"site/somethingsweetsomethingsavoury-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"somethingsweetsomethingsavoury.com","note":"Scottish home baking and cooking section of a UK food blog.","url":"https://somethingsweetsomethingsavoury.com/category/scottish-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Scottish cuisine","links":[["Cock-a-leekie","dish/cock-a-leekie"],["Finnan haddie","dish/finnan-haddie"],["Haggis","dish/haggis"],["Haggis pakora","dish/haggis-pakora"],["Hobnob biscuit","dish/hobnob-biscuit"],["Lorne sausage","dish/lorne-sausage"],["Mince and tatties","dish/mince-and-tatties"],["Rumbledethumps","dish/rumbledethumps"],["Scotch broth","dish/scotch-broth"],["Skirlie","dish/skirlie"],["Stornoway black pudding","dish/stornoway-black-pudding"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/bf/Clootie_dumpling.jpg/500px-Clootie_dumpling.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Matt Riggott from Reykjavik, Iceland","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clootie_dumpling.jpg"}},"ingredient/white-meat":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/white-meat","n":"White meat","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["In culinary terms, white meat is meat which is pale in color before and after cooking. In traditional gastronomy, white meat also includes rabbit, the flesh of milk-fed young mammals (in particular veal and lamb), and sometimes pork. In culinary contexts, the term is employed loosely, and not used in perfect contrast to the nutritional category of red meat."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-white-meat"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_meat"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Fricassee","dish/fricassee"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Fricassee","dish/fricassee"]]]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/42/Chicken_breasts_and_wing_quarters.jpg/500px-Chicken_breasts_and_wing_quarters.jpg","w":500,"h":281,"by":"Harald the Bard","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chicken_breasts_and_wing_quarters.jpg"}},"dish/gyokuro":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/gyokuro","n":"Gyokuro","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Japanese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Gyokuro is a type of green tea from Japan. It differs from the standard sencha (a classic green tea grown in the sun) in being grown under the shade rather than the full sun. The name \"gyokuro\" translates as \"jewel dew\" (or \"jade dew\"). According to the Japan Tea Central Association, gyokuro is defined as \"a tea manufactured in the same manner as sencha from tea leaves picked from covered tea gardens that are almost completely shaded from sunlight for about 20 days using covering materials such as reed screens, straw, or shading nets, from the time when the new shoots of the first flush start to grow.\""]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-gyokuro"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gyokuro"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Just One Cookbook","ref":"site/justonecookbook-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"justonecookbook.com","note":"Namiko Chen's long-running blog and videos on Japanese home cooking.","url":"https://justonecookbook.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"RecipeTin Japan","ref":"site/japan-recipetineats-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"japan.recipetineats.com","note":"Japanese home recipes fr